{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-design.review.md\ncat reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.report.md'","aggregated_output":"<!-- Reviewer: Codex, requested gpt-6.2-astra xhigh; observed gpt-6.2-astra, effort xhigh (session rollout turn_context). Read-only design review of the rehearsal harness at ae174bd. Same provider as the harness's builder (gpt-6.2-sol): not a cross-provider review. Saved by the coordinator. -->\n\n**DX12 remains unverified.** All four expected tables are correct. The two material gaps are that a line can bridge missing days and pass, and a clean transcript does not establish that the agent had access only to the published materials. The coordinator reports 31/31 harness tests passing, including browser grading. No agent has taken the rehearsal. [DX12](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/docs/02-product-spec.md:234) · [Coordinator’s report](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/reviews/2026-11-19/wp/rehearsal-harness.report.md:1)\n\n### Fixture validity\n\nAll five inputs are plausible back-end JSON. I independently reconstructed every expected row from the source dates, keys and decimal values without running Axylo. The comparisons matched for visits (27 observations over 30 days), revenue (7 over 8), signups (7) and sales (4 quarters × 3 regions).\n\n| Trap | What a first attempt would do |\n|---|---|\n| UTC-midnight ISO dates | A genuine discriminator in visits and revenue. With plain `x: 'day'`, March 1 reads as February 28 in Los Angeles and March 1 in Tokyo. AX111 is a hint. Declaring `type: 'date'` or supplying calendar-date strings fixes the reading. |\n| Numeric strings | Regional sales passes untouched. Every regional field contains only decimal strings, so Axylo converts them and emits hint AX104. Revenue is the discriminating case: its five strings mixed with two numbers are omitted with AX106. Converting the decimals before charting preserves all seven values. `parse: 'number'` alone does not fix a mixed field. |\n| Unsorted rows | Visits and revenue are genuinely shuffled. Axylo silently sorts dated rows and keeps each value with its day. This exercises the library default and gives no evidence the agent learned to sort. |\n| Missing days | March 6, 12 and 26 and May 4 have no rows. Both series meet the regular-step rule, so Axylo infers gaps with hint AX105. Filling them with zero fails. Sorting the observations and plotting their dates as categories can pass while drawing a continuous line across every omission. |\n| Misspelled field | Asking for `signups` as the field raises AX101. Inspecting the JSON or following the diagnostic finds `signpus`. The passing reference answer uses that key without a label, so readers see the typo. `label: 'Signups'` fixes it. |\n\nThese are predictions from the documented behavior and harness tests. A pass measures the delivered chart, including the benefit of good defaults. It cannot establish which documentation the agent needed. [Data guide](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/apps/docs/pages/data.md:47) · [Browser grading cases](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/test/grade.browser.test.mjs:25)\n\n### What the grade establishes\n\nThe grader checks production console and handle diagnostics, detects an error state, and allows ordinary warnings and hints. It catches a bar substituted for a line, a shortened accessible name, and missing or swapped regional values. Requiring the requested title is justified even though A1 permits a generated fallback. A visually clipped title with an intact accessible name passes. [Grader](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/grade.mjs:122)\n\nI interpret “reader criteria” as the applicable outcomes on the delivered chart. A1/A4 cover its name, caption, headers, units and values. F2/F4/F11 cover the *drawn* gaps, calendar days and order. F12 covers visible and accessible disclosure of omissions or changes. The grader checks only part of this. I directly verified that its comparator accepts visits labelled `Temperature (kg)` and rejects the correct date `06 April 2026`. It ignores time text after a recognized date. Its note check matches one English phrase, and every fixture expects no omission note. If agent code filters out a valid revenue row, the original-fixture comparison **does** fail, even though Axylo never saw the row and cannot disclose its loss. [Table comparator](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/table.mjs:18) · [Note check](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/grade.mjs:138)\n\n### Must change before counting runs, in priority order\n\n1. **Enforce docs-only access.** In `workspace.mjs` and `agent.mjs`, give each attempt a disposable home and filesystem with read-only published inputs, writable answer space, no repository or earlier sessions, and network access limited to model transport. Disable web tools, connectors and persistent memory. The four prompts are neutral and identical across families. The execution conditions differ: Codex gets `workspace-write`, Claude gets tool permissions including Node, and only Codex receives the requested effort. In `audit.mjs`, treat incomplete logs as unverifiable. I confirmed both empty and malformed input return `void: false`. Transcript path matching cannot establish what a subprocess read or fetched. [Launcher](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/agent.mjs:9) · [Audit](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/audit.mjs:141)\n2. **Grade the reader’s chart.** In `grade.mjs`, `table.mjs` and `test/grade.browser.test.mjs`, check the caption and semantic headers for every series, correct the signup reference label, and accept equivalent unambiguous date formats. Check plotted order and breaks at the four known missing dates, sample a reader date in both zones, and check clipping when a title is visible. Add controls for a bridged categorical line, agent-filtered revenue and a genuine omission whose note must have the right count in the accessibility tree and on screen. The repository already has a painted-gap check to reuse. [Existing F2 test](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/f2-gaps.browser.test.ts:1)\n3. **Freeze the release rule in `README.md`, `run.mjs` and `summary.mjs`.** My proposed minimum is three fresh, uncoached attempts per task with Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code Opus 5.5, pinned to observed model versions. That gives 24 attempts. Allow documented self-checks within each 20-minute session, with no grader feedback. Require 3/3 in every model–task cell for a 1.0 rehearsal pass. Keep failures and timeouts. Record and replace only documented voids or infrastructure failures. Save CLI versions, effective effort, input and grader hashes, and per-cell counts. Three successes give a two-sided exact 95% interval of roughly 29–100% per cell under independent runs. Report the observed counts as release rehearsal evidence limited to these prompts, models, line/bar charts and Chrome environments. They establish neither a general pass rate nor human usability.\n\n**Would be better.** Shuffle the quarters in `regional-sales.json` and derive chronological quarter order independently in `fixtures.test.mjs`. Categories retain encounter order, so this would add an order mistake an agent can actually make. A second grading input with changed values would also distinguish a working `render(element, data)` from a hard-coded answer.\n\nThis was a read-only review. I ran the independent fixture comparisons and comparator/audit probes. The browser test count comes from the coordinator. No contract change is needed.<!-- Author: Codex, requested gpt-6.2-sol xhigh; observed gpt-6.2-sol, effort xhigh (session rollout turn_context). workspace-write. Verified by the coordinator: npx vitest run --root rehearsal, 31 of 31 pass including the 15 browser grading cases the sandbox could not run. Design by the coordinator (Claude Opus 5.5); design review by gpt-6.2-astra follows. Saved by the coordinator. -->\n\nImplemented DX12 in [rehearsal/](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/). It includes four fixed prompts and fixtures with hand-written expected rows, a [runner](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/run.mjs) for Codex and Claude, a [grader](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/grade.mjs) for Los Angeles and Tokyo, and a [summary](/home/canarit/projects/axylo/rehearsal/summary.mjs) of saved records.\n\n```sh\nnode rehearsal/run.mjs --agent codex --model YOUR_MODEL_ID --effort high \\\n  --task daily-visits --workdir /tmp/axylo-runs\nnode rehearsal/summary.mjs\n```\n\nA local stub verified invocation, transcript capture, model recording, and the summary. The non-browser tests passed.\n\n```text\nTest Files  2 passed (2)\n     Tests  16 passed (16)\n```\n\nChrome failed to launch in the sandbox with `setsockopt: Operation not permitted`, so the 15 browser cases remain unverified. No live agent runs were made.\n\nThe visits fixture covers 30 calendar days with 27 observations. The grader accepts each missing day as absent or “No data.” Axylo automatically parses fields made entirely of decimal strings, so the unparsed-string failure test uses mixed numbers and strings in the combined fixture. The workspace copy preserves published examples and omits internal source snapshots. The audit checks paths visible in transcripts. The harness uses the existing public contracts.","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
