{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'codex exec --help\nclaude --help\ncodex --version\nclaude --version'","aggregated_output":"WARNING: proceeding, even though we could not create PATH aliases: Read-only file system (os error 30)\nRun Codex non-interactively\n\nUsage: codex exec [OPTIONS] [PROMPT]\n       codex exec [OPTIONS] <COMMAND> [ARGS]\n\nCommands:\n  resume  Resume a previous session by id or pick the most recent with --last\n  fork    Fork a previous session by id into a new session\n  review  Run a code review against the current repository\n  help    Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)\n\nArguments:\n  [PROMPT]\n          Initial instructions for the agent. If not provided as an argument (or if `-` is used),\n          instructions are read from stdin. If stdin is piped and a prompt is also provided, stdin\n          is appended as a `<stdin>` block\n\nOptions:\n  -c, --config <key=value>\n          Override a configuration value that would otherwise be loaded from `~/.codex/config.toml`.\n          Use a dotted path (`foo.bar.baz`) to override nested values. The `value` portion is parsed\n          as TOML. If it fails to parse as TOML, the raw string is used as a literal.\n          \n          Examples: - `-c model=\"o3\"` - `-c 'sandbox_permissions=[\"disk-full-read-access\"]'` - `-c\n          shell_environment_policy.inherit=all`\n\n      --enable <FEATURE>\n          Enable a feature (repeatable). Equivalent to `-c features.<name>=true`\n\n      --disable <FEATURE>\n          Disable a feature (repeatable). Equivalent to `-c features.<name>=false`\n\n      --strict-config\n          Error out when config.toml contains fields that are not recognized by this version of\n          Codex\n\n  -i, --image <FILE>...\n          Optional image(s) to attach to the initial prompt\n\n  -m, --model <MODEL>\n          Model the agent should use\n\n      --oss\n          Use open-source provider\n\n      --local-provider <OSS_PROVIDER>\n          Specify which local provider to use (lmstudio or ollama). If not specified with --oss,\n          will use config default or show selection\n\n  -p, --profile <CONFIG_PROFILE_V2>\n          Layer $CODEX_HOME/<name>.config.toml on top of the base user config\n\n  -s, --sandbox <SANDBOX_MODE>\n          Select the sandbox policy to use when executing model-generated shell commands\n          \n          [possible values: read-only, workspace-write, danger-full-access]\n\n      --approve-for-me\n          Route approval requests through automatic review using the workspace-write sandbox\n\n      --dangerously-bypass-approvals-and-sandbox\n          Skip all confirmation prompts and execute commands without sandboxing. EXTREMELY\n          DANGEROUS. Intended solely for running in environments that are externally sandboxed\n\n      --dangerously-bypass-hook-trust\n          Run enabled hooks without requiring persisted hook trust for this invocation. DANGEROUS.\n          Intended only for automation that already vets hook sources\n\n  -C, --cd <DIR>\n          Tell the agent to use the specified directory as its working root\n\n      --worktree\n          Run the session in a new managed Git worktree\n\n      --add-dir <DIR>\n          Additional directories that should be writable alongside the primary workspace\n\n      --thread-source <SOURCE>\n          Source classification for newly created or forked threads\n\n      --cyber-access-program <PROGRAM>\n          Request an experimental Cyber access program for this turn (OpenAI provider only). Omit to\n          use server defaults. Not supported with review; fork requires a prompt\n          \n          [possible values: standard, daybreak_blue, daybreak_red]\n\n      --skip-git-repo-check\n          Allow running Codex outside a Git repository\n\n      --trust\n          Save trust for this project and future chats, including MCP commands outside the sandbox.\n          Persists even with --ephemeral. Does not override an explicitly untrusted project\n\n      --ephemeral\n          Run without persisting session files to disk\n\n      --ignore-user-config\n          Do not load `$CODEX_HOME/config.toml`; auth still uses `CODEX_HOME`\n\n      --ignore-rules\n          Do not load user or project execpolicy `.rules` files\n\n      --output-schema <FILE>\n          Path to a JSON Schema file describing the model's final response shape\n\n      --color <COLOR>\n          Specifies color settings for use in the output\n          \n          [default: auto]\n          [possible values: always, never, auto]\n\n      --json\n          Print events to stdout as JSONL\n\n  -o, --output-last-message <FILE>\n          Specifies file where the last message from the agent should be written\n\n  -h, --help\n          Print help (see a summary with '-h')\n\n  -V, --version\n          Print version\nUsage: claude [options] [command] [prompt]\n\nClaude Code - starts an interactive session by default, use -p/--print for\nnon-interactive output\n\nArguments:\n  prompt                                Your prompt\n\nOptions:\n  --add-dir <directories...>            More directories Claude can access\n  --agent <agent>                       Agent for the current session. Overrides\n                                        the 'agent' setting.\n  --agents <json-or-file>               JSON object defining custom agents, or\n                                        with --print the path to a file that\n                                        holds one (e.g. '{\"reviewer\":\n                                        {\"description\": \"Reviews code\",\n                                        \"prompt\": \"You are a code reviewer\"}}')\n  --allow-dangerously-skip-permissions  Enable bypassing all permission checks\n                                        as an option, without it being enabled\n                                        by default. Recommended only for\n                                        sandboxes with no internet access.\n  --allowedTools, --allowed-tools <tools...>\n      Comma or space-separated list of tool names to allow (e.g. \"Bash(git *)\n      Edit\")\n  --append-system-prompt <prompt>       Add text to the end of the default\n                                        system prompt\n  --autocompact <auto|tokens>           Auto-compact window size (auto, or at\n                                        least 100k tokens)\n  --ax-screen-reader                    Render screen-reader friendly output\n                                        (flat text, no decorative borders or\n                                        animations).\n  --bg, --background                    Start the session in the background and\n                                        return immediately. Prints the id that\n                                        `claude attach`, `logs`, `stop` and `rm`\n                                        take; `claude agents` lists them. With\n                                        --resume <session-id>, continues that\n                                        session in the background under the same\n                                        ID, or starts a copy and says so when\n                                        the session is already running\n  --bare                                Minimal mode: skip hooks (those defined\n                                        in settings and by installed plugins;\n                                        features built into Claude Code and the\n                                        hooks of your organization's managed\n                                        plugins are unaffected), LSP, plugin\n                                        sync, attribution, auto-memory,\n                                        background prefetches, keychain reads,\n                                        and CLAUDE.md auto-discovery. Sets\n                                        CLAUDE_CODE_SIMPLE=1. Anthropic auth is\n                                        strictly ANTHROPIC_API_KEY or\n                                        apiKeyHelper via --settings (OAuth and\n                                        keychain are never read). Third-party\n                                        providers (Bedrock/Vertex/Foundry) use\n                                        their own credentials. Skills still\n                                        resolve via /skill-name. Explicitly\n                                        provide context via:\n                                        --system-prompt[-file],\n                                        --append-system-prompt[-file], --add-dir\n                                        (CLAUDE.md dirs), --mcp-config,\n                                        --settings, --agents, --plugin-dir.\n  --betas <betas...>                    Beta headers to include in API requests\n                                        (API key users only)\n  --brief                               Turn on the SendUserMessage tool, which\n                                        Claude uses to message you\n  --chrome                              Turn on Claude in Chrome\n  --cloud [description|session_id|url]  Create a cloud session with the given\n                                        description, or attach to an existing\n                                        one by session ID or claude.ai/code URL\n  -c, --continue                        Continue the most recent conversation in\n                                        the current directory\n  --dangerously-skip-permissions        Bypass all permission checks.\n                                        Recommended only for sandboxes with no\n                                        internet access.\n  -d, --debug [filter]                  Enable debug mode with optional category\n                                        filtering (e.g., --debug=hooks,mcp or\n                                        --debug='!hooks,!mcp')\n  --debug-file <path>                   Write debug logs to a specific file path\n                                        (implicitly enables debug mode)\n  --desktop                             Open in the Claude Desktop app instead\n                                        of the terminal (with --continue or\n                                        --resume <id> to pick the session)\n  --disable-slash-commands              Disable all skills and commands\n  --disallowedTools, --disallowed-tools <tools...>\n      Comma or space-separated list of tool names to deny (e.g. \"Bash(git *)\n      Edit\")\n  --effort <level>                      Effort level for the current session\n                                        (low, medium, high, xhigh, max)\n  --environment <environment_id>        Create a new cloud session that runs on\n                                        the given self-hosted environment\n                                        (ccpool_...).\n  --exclude-dynamic-system-prompt-sections\n      Move per-machine sections (cwd, env info, memory paths, git status) from\n      the system prompt into the first user message. Improves cross-user\n      prompt-cache reuse. Only applies with the default system prompt (ignored\n      with --system-prompt). (default: false)\n  --fallback-model <model>              Models to fall back to when the main\n                                        model is busy or unavailable\n                                        (comma-separated, in order). The main\n                                        model is tried again at the start of\n                                        each turn.\n  --file <specs...>                     Files to download at startup. Format:\n                                        file_id:relative_path (e.g., --file\n                                        file_abc:doc.txt file_def:img.png)\n  --fork-session                        When resuming, create a new session ID\n                                        instead of reusing the original (use\n                                        with --resume or --continue)\n  --forward-subagent-text               Forward subagent text and thinking\n                                        blocks as assistant/user messages with\n                                        parent_tool_use_id set (only works with\n                                        --print and --output-format=stream-json)\n  --from-pr [value]                     Resume a session linked to a PR by\n                                        number or URL, or pick from a list\n                                        (optionally with a search term)\n  -h, --help                            Display help for command\n  --ide                                 Automatically connect to IDE on startup\n                                        if exactly one valid IDE is available\n  --include-hook-events                 Include all hook lifecycle events in the\n                                        output stream (only works with\n                                        --output-format=stream-json)\n  --include-partial-messages            Include partial message chunks as they\n                                        arrive (only works with --print and\n                                        --output-format=stream-json)\n  --input-format <format>               Input format (only works with --print):\n                                        \"text\" (default), or \"stream-json\"\n                                        (realtime streaming input) (choices:\n                                        \"text\", \"stream-json\")\n  --json-schema <schema>                JSON Schema for structured output\n                                        validation (only works with --print).\n                                        Example:\n                                        {\"type\":\"object\",\"properties\":{\"name\":{\"type\":\"string\"}},\"required\":[\"name\"]}\n  --max-budget-usd <amount>             Maximum dollar amount to spend on API\n                                        calls (only works with --print)\n  --mcp-config <configs...>             Load MCP servers from JSON files or\n                                        strings (space-separated)\n  --model <model>                       Model for the current session. Use an\n                                        alias for the latest model (e.g.\n                                        'fable', 'opus', or 'sonnet') or a\n                                        model's full name.\n  -n, --name <name>                     Set a display name for this session\n                                        (shown in the prompt box, /resume\n                                        picker, and terminal title)\n  --no-chrome                           Turn off Claude in Chrome\n  --no-session-persistence              Don't save sessions to disk; they can't\n                                        be resumed (only works with --print)\n  --output-format <format>              Output format (only works with --print):\n                                        \"text\" (default), \"json\" (single\n                                        result), or \"stream-json\" (realtime\n                                        streaming) (choices: \"text\", \"json\",\n                                        \"stream-json\")\n  --permission-mode <mode>              Permission mode to use for the session\n                                        (choices: \"acceptEdits\", \"auto\",\n                                        \"bypassPermissions\", \"manual\",\n                                        \"dontAsk\", \"plan\")\n  --permission-prompts <target>         Who answers permission prompts with\n                                        --print: \"host\" (the SDK host or\n                                        --permission-prompt-tool) or \"none\"\n                                        (nobody: anything that would prompt is\n                                        denied automatically; the permission\n                                        mode still decides everything else)\n                                        (choices: \"host\", \"none\", default:\n                                        \"host\")\n  --plugin-dir <path>                   Load a plugin from a directory or .zip\n                                        for this session only; a folder of\n                                        plugins loads each child (repeatable:\n                                        --plugin-dir A --plugin-dir B.zip)\n                                        (default: [])\n  --plugin-url <url>                    Fetch a plugin .zip from a URL for this\n                                        session only (repeatable: --plugin-url A\n                                        --plugin-url B) (default: [])\n  -p, --print                           Print response and exit (useful for\n                                        pipes). Note: The workspace trust dialog\n                                        is skipped when Claude is run in\n                                        non-interactive mode (via -p, or when\n                                        stdout is not a TTY, e.g. piped or\n                                        redirected output). Only use this in\n                                        directories you trust. Settings files\n                                        that fail validation are silently\n                                        ignored in this mode (no error dialog is\n                                        shown).\n  --prompt-suggestions [value]          Turn on prompt suggestions. In print/SDK\n                                        mode, sends a prompt_suggestion message\n                                        after each turn with a predicted next\n                                        user prompt (choices: \"true\", \"false\",\n                                        \"1\", \"0\", \"yes\", \"no\", \"on\", \"off\",\n                                        preset: \"true\")\n  --remote-control [name]               Start an interactive session with Remote\n                                        Control enabled (optionally named)\n  --remote-control-session-name-prefix <prefix>\n      Prefix for auto-generated Remote Control session names (default: hostname)\n  --replay-user-messages                Re-emit user messages from stdin back on\n                                        stdout for acknowledgment (only works\n                                        with --input-format=stream-json and\n                                        --output-format=stream-json)\n  --restricted                          Restricted mode: removes the built-in\n                                        tools that run commands or code (Bash,\n                                        PowerShell and the other code-running\n                                        tools) and WebFetch unless --tools names\n                                        them, and ignores user, project and\n                                        local settings files (managed settings\n                                        and --settings still apply; add\n                                        --strict-mcp-config to skip MCP servers\n                                        too). Also confines the file tools to\n                                        the working directories (--add-dir\n                                        included), refuses bypassPermissions,\n                                        and lets only a person or the configured\n                                        permission handler approve writes to\n                                        settings, git and tool-configuration\n                                        files.\n  -r, --resume [value]                  Resume a conversation by session ID or\n                                        name, or pick from a list (optionally\n                                        with a search term)\n  --safe-mode                           Start with all customizations\n                                        (CLAUDE.md, skills, installed plugins,\n                                        hooks, MCP servers, custom commands and\n                                        agents, output styles, workflows, custom\n                                        themes, keybindings, and more) disabled\n                                        — useful for troubleshooting a broken\n                                        configuration. Admin-managed (policy)\n                                        settings and the hooks of managed\n                                        plugins still apply. Auth, model\n                                        selection, built-in tools and plugins,\n                                        and permissions work normally. Sets\n                                        CLAUDE_CODE_SAFE_MODE=1.\n  --session-id <uuid>                   Use a specific session ID for the\n                                        conversation (must be a valid UUID)\n  --setting-sources <sources>           Comma-separated list of setting sources\n                                        to load (user, project, local).\n  --settings <file-or-json>             Settings file or JSON string to load\n                                        more settings from\n  --strict-mcp-config                   Only use MCP servers from --mcp-config,\n                                        ignoring all other MCP configurations\n  --system-prompt <prompt>              System prompt to use for the session\n  --system-prompt-snapshot <on|off>     Record the system prompt once per\n                                        conversation and reuse it verbatim on\n                                        every request and resume. on (the\n                                        default, except under --bare outside\n                                        cloud and background sessions): the\n                                        prompt is rendered on the conversation's\n                                        first request — a --system-prompt or\n                                        --append-system-prompt included — sent,\n                                        and recorded; every later request and\n                                        resume sends the record as-is, even when\n                                        a later launch passes different text,\n                                        until the conversation is compacted.\n                                        off: never record; the prompt is\n                                        rendered fresh every request (for\n                                        iterating on prompt text). (choices:\n                                        \"on\", \"off\")\n  --teleport [session]                  Resume a teleport session, optionally\n                                        specify session ID\n  --tmux                                Create a tmux session for the worktree\n                                        (requires --worktree). Uses iTerm2\n                                        native panes when available; use\n                                        --tmux=classic for traditional tmux.\n  --tools <tools...>                    Built-in tools to make available: \"\" for\n                                        none, \"default\" for the default set, or\n                                        tool names (e.g. \"Bash,Edit,Read\")\n  --verbose                             Override the verbose setting\n  -v, --version                         Output the version number\n  -w, --worktree [name]                 Create a git worktree for this session\n                                        (optionally named)\n\nCommands:\n  agents [options]                      Manage background agents\n  attach <id|name>                      Open a background session in this\n                                        terminal. <id> is the short id that\n                                        `claude --bg` prints and `claude agents`\n                                        lists. Part of the session name works\n                                        too\n  auth                                  Manage authentication\n  auto-mode                             Inspect or reset auto mode configuration\n  doctor                                Check the health of your Claude Code\n                                        installation. Reads settings files in\n                                        the current directory without a trust\n                                        prompt. For a full checkup that can also\n                                        fix issues, run /doctor in a session.\n  gateway [options]                     Run the enterprise auth/telemetry\n                                        gateway\n  import [options] [source]             Import config from another AI coding\n                                        agent into Claude Code\n  install [options] [target]            Install the Claude Code native build.\n                                        Use [target] to pick a version (stable,\n                                        latest, or a version number)\n  logs <id|name>                        Print a background session's recent\n                                        terminal output\n  mcp                                   Configure and manage MCP servers\n  plugin|plugins                        Manage Claude Code plugins\n  purge [options] [path]                Delete a project's Claude Code data\n                                        (transcripts, tasks, file history, and\n                                        its entry in ~/.claude.json) from this\n                                        computer only\n  respawn [options] [id]                Restart a background session, or all of\n                                        them with --all, so it runs the current\n                                        Claude Code version\n  rm <id>                               If it's safe, remove a background\n                                        session from the list and delete its\n                                        worktree. Its conversation is kept:\n                                        `claude --resume` lists it. Works on\n                                        sessions that have already exited\n  setup-token                           Set up a long-lived authentication token\n                                        (requires Claude subscription)\n  stop|kill <id>                        Stop a background session. Its\n                                        conversation is kept: `claude attach\n                                        <id>` opens it again, `claude --resume`\n                                        works once it is stopped\n  ultrareview [options] [target]        Run a cloud-hosted multi-agent code\n                                        review of the current branch (or a PR\n                                        number / base branch) and print the\n                                        findings\n  update|upgrade                        Check for updates and install if\n                                        available; package-manager installs\n                                        update through the package manager\nWARNING: proceeding, even though we could not create PATH aliases: Read-only file system (os error 30)\ncodex-cli 0.170.0\n2.1.327 (Claude Code)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '89,111p' docs/02-product-spec.md\nsed -n '133,148p' docs/02-product-spec.md\nrg -n 'Values not shown|axylo-note|axylo-title|aria-valuetext|titleHidden|table.caption|createCaption|columnheader|rowheader|scene path|series-color-' packages/axylo/src packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance | head -110\ncat package.json\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '89,111p' docs/02-product-spec.md\nsed -n '133,148p' docs/02-product-spec.md\nrg -n 'Values not shown|axylo-note|axylo-title|aria-valuetext|titleHidden|table.caption|createCaption|columnheader|rowheader|scene path|series-color-' packages/axylo/src packages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance | head -110\ncat package.json\"","aggregated_output":"Every criterion maps to at least one test in the acceptance matrix kept in the repository (criterion → test → chart types it applies to → result). \"Property test\" means the statement is checked over at least 1,000 generated datasets. Expected values come from the fixture data and this specification, never from the implementation's output.\n\n### 3.1 Faithful representation\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | Bar and area value axes always include zero. An author `domain` that excludes zero is widened and raises AX110. | Property test on domains, including all-negative and mixed-sign data; `bars` fixture (values 82–97) starts at 0 | R1 |\n| F2 | Missing values are never drawn as zero and never bridged unless the author asks. An explicit `null` is a gap, silently. An isolated value between two gaps is a visible point. A missing bar shows a \"no data\" mark at the baseline, distinct from a zero bar; a slot a series merely has no row for gets no bar and no mark. On a time or date axis with a regular step, an absent step is a gap and raises AX105; `missing: 'zero'` on the field turns absent and null values into zeros for counts. Tooltip, table and announcements say \"No data\". | `trend` with nulls and `trend` with the same 7 rows deleted render the same gap; `dirty` fixtures; property test that no segment connects across a missing index | R5 |\n| F3 | Input that cannot be plotted as given is reported, never guessed. The diagnostic registry (section 3.5) fixes each case's level, what is drawn and what the reader sees. | One test per code; the combined `dirty` fixture and the four one-bad-value fixtures yield exactly the codes listed in section 3.5 | R5, D5 |\n| F4 | Three temporal types, never mixed silently. **Calendar dates** (`2026-02-24`, `2026-02`, `2026`) read the same in every viewer time zone. **Instants** (epoch milliseconds declared `type: 'time'`, `Date` objects, ISO strings with an offset) are shown in `timeZone` (default: the viewer's), with the zone named in the tooltip heading and the description. **Wall-clock times** (date-time strings without an offset) are shown as written, with hint AX112. Instants that all fall on the same time of day, whole days apart, raise hint AX111 (\"these look like calendar dates\"). A bare number is never assumed to be an instant. | Four fixture variants in UTC, America/Los_Angeles and Asia/Kolkata: (a) date strings: identical text in all three; (b) ISO strings with `Z`, (c) epoch ms with `type: 'time'`, (d) `Date` objects: each zone's correct local text, zone visible, AX111 raised; with `timeZone: 'UTC'` or `type: 'date'`: identical. Plus: both DST transitions, a leap day, an unparseable date | R3 |\n| F5 | No smoothing. When a line is denser than the screen, the drawing is simplified with this guarantee: for every device-pixel column, and every contiguous run of valid samples inside it, the drawn stroke covers exactly that run's minimum and maximum. A break between two runs is always kept, even when both fall in one pixel column; a slot a series merely has no row for (weekly data beside daily) does not break its line, except inside a stack (F10). Stacked areas are simplified on their cumulative sums. Exact values always come from the data, not the drawing. | Assertions on which source samples the drawing kept, for: one outlier in 1,000,000; two peaks and a gap in one column; alternating valid and missing samples; tied extremes; device pixel ratio 1 and 2; several zoom levels. A sample that the drawing dropped is still reachable by keyboard and in the table | D8, D9 |\n| F6 | Numbers and dates are formatted for the page locale. Ticks and readouts are formatted separately: ticks may be compact (`50K`); tooltip, table and announcements show the full integer part, and the fraction with up to six significant digits not counting the fraction's leading zeros (`123,456.75`; `0.000123456`; `0.1 + 0.2` reads `0.3`), with the unit. A custom formatter that throws falls back to the default and raises a diagnostic. | Expected strings for chosen values (large integers, small decimals, percentages, zoned times) in `en-US`, `de-DE` and `en-IN` | R4 |\n| F7 | Domains follow one order of precedence: the zoomed view is inside the author's `domain` if given, otherwise inside the automatic domain. This rule is for value axes: the y axis of line, area and bar, and both axes of scatter. A continuous x axis of line, area or bar spans exactly its data and any x reference the chart draws (plus half a band for bars), so a time series is not padded to the next tick; so does a date or time x axis of a scatter, whose numeric x alone follows the tick rule below. After a zoom on x, a y axis with no author domain is fitted again to the values in view, by the same rules. The automatic domain contains all data, the reference lines and bands the chart draws (a compact chart draws none, so `reference` has no effect there and is reported as an option that does not apply) and stack totals, and ends at the first tick at or beyond each extreme (a compact chart draws no ticks, so its domain is the same one without being shown to end on a tick). A field declared `scale: 'percent'` (0–100) or `'ratio'` (0–1) ends at full scale only when its maximum is at least half of full scale and not above it. Nothing is inferred from a label. | Property tests; `bars` ends at 100 when declared `percent`; a 4% maximum ends at the first tick at or above 4% | R1 |\n| F8 | Donut: slices keep the given order, start at 12 o'clock and run clockwise. The legend shows every slice's value and percentage as text, in slice order. A zero slice is listed without an arc; a `null` slice is listed as \"No data\". A negative value is AX120 and the chart shows its error state. With more than eight slices that have an arc (a positive value), the seven largest are drawn and the rest of those are grouped as \"Other (n)\", with AX121; zero and `null` slices never count toward the limit and are never grouped, so each stays listed on its own; the table lists every category. Shares are shown with at most one fraction digit. An all-zero donut shows the empty state. | `parts` fixture; one test per case | R7, landscape scoreboard 3.1, WCAG 1.4.1 |\n| F9 | Up to eight series, no two share a color. Beyond eight, each series still has a unique combination of color and dash or marker (for bars, full and compact alike: color and fill pattern; for every chart type at least 32 distinct combinations, after which combinations repeat), and AX130 is raised. | Tests with 6, 8 and 12 series | R7 |\n| F10 | Stacks place positive and negative values on their own sides of zero, and the axis covers both totals. The readout's total is the sum when every component has the same sign; when signs are mixed it gives the positive total and the negative total, the two ends the reader sees, not their net. A missing component is drawn with zero height and the stack is marked \"incomplete\" in tooltip, table and announcements. A component is missing whenever its series has no value at that x, whatever the reason: a null, an absent step, or a series that simply has no row there (a weekly series stacked with a daily one is incomplete on the days between its rows; the owner's decision, 2026-11-18: a stack must never show a total made of values the data does not contain). A stacked series' line follows its band, so inside a stack it is not joined across such a slot. | Property test on stacked geometry; mixed-sign and incomplete fixtures | R5 |\n| F11 | Line, area and scatter are drawn in x order whatever the input order. On line, area and bar, two rows with the same x in one series are AX108 and that series is not drawn. On scatter, points sharing an x (or both coordinates) are ordinary data. | Shuffled `trend` renders identically; duplicate fixture | R5 |\n| F12 | When a warning means values were left out or changed, the chart says so to the reader: a short note (\"Values not shown: 2. See table.\", worded so it reads correctly for any count) inside the figure and in the accessibility tree, in every build. | Production build given `dirty` shows the note | R5, R6 |\n\n### 3.2 Legibility\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n\nTarget: every WCAG 2.2 level AA success criterion that applies to what Axylo renders, as component-level evidence. Conformance of a page is a property of the page and remains the author's.\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A1 | Every chart has an accessible name: its `title`, or the heading named by `labelledBy`, or else a generated name (\"Line chart of Sessions by Day\") with AX140 raised in every build. The title's heading level is the author's (`level`), and it can be hidden visually. Every chart has a generated summary: type, series, x range with time zone, y range, each series' minimum, maximum and last value, and any omissions. `description` adds to the summary and never removes the zone or omission statements. Element ids are unique per chart. | Accessibility-tree snapshot for every fixture; a 24-chart page has no duplicate ids; axe `heading-order` | R11, R13, WCAG 1.1.1, 1.3.1, 2.4.6 |\n| A2 | Keyboard: at most two tab stops while the table is closed, the plot and one toolbar with roving focus that holds legend toggles, zoom, pause and the table control. Every point is reachable in order; the series can be switched; the ends can be reached directly; focus can leave the chart. The focus indicator is at least 2 px and 3:1 and is not hidden by the tooltip. The exact keys and roles are fixed after the pattern spike in M1 (section 8). | Forward and reverse traversal in collapsed, zoomed, streaming and table-open states; focus after hiding the focused series; indicator contrast | R12, WCAG 2.1.1, 2.1.2, 2.4.3, 2.4.7, 2.4.11, 1.4.11 |\n| A3 | Moving focus to a point conveys its text (series, x, value with unit, position) through the focused element's value text: the plot is a native range control whose `aria-valuetext` describes the focused point (decision 0001; earlier drafts said \"accessible name or a polite live region\", which the chosen pattern does not use, and A7 forbids announcements for navigation other than this). On touch screen readers, every point is reachable by swiping, or the table is reachable within two gestures. | Accessibility-tree snapshot after each key. No screen-reader rehearsal is part of 1.0 (the owner's decision, 2026-11-18): what a reader actually speaks on NVDA, VoiceOver or TalkBack has not been checked, and the conformance table says so. The script for such a rehearsal is kept in decision 0001 for when one is done. | R12, R13, WCAG 4.1.2 |\n| A4 | \"View as table\" shows the data as an HTML table built from the normalized data when opened: the title as caption, field labels with units as column headers, x values as row headers, values formatted as in F6. It covers the current x range, 100 rows per page, with every row reachable by paging or by jumping to an x value. It sits in a focusable scroll region no taller than the plot, and opening it does not move other charts. | Table content compared with fixture data, including a value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000; a 24-chart page layout test | R13, WCAG 1.3.1, 1.3.2, 3.3.1 |\n| A5 | Series are never distinguished by color alone, in any mode. Each line and area carries a shape marker at its last point and at sparse intervals, repeated in the legend and tooltip; under `prefers-contrast: more` or forced colors, lines also differ by dash. Adjacent filled marks (slices, stacked segments) are separated by at least 1 px of background. Bars are told apart by position and order, and by the legend and tooltip that name them; a compact chart has neither, so there neighbouring bar series also differ by fill pattern (series further apart may share a pattern: four patterns exist). The default palette has eight colors, each at least 3:1 on the default light and dark backgrounds, with a minimum pairwise OKLab difference of 0.10 (provisional) under normal vision and simulated protanopia, deuteranopia and tritanopia. On any other background, Axylo measures the palette against the resolved background and raises AX141 below 3:1. | Palette script scoring the rendered marks of an eight-series chart in both schemes, repeats included; rendered-pixel checks of separators; a grayscale screenshot in which three crossing lines can be told apart by marker | R7, WCAG 1.4.1, 1.4.11 |\n| A6 | Content reflows at 320 CSS px and 400% zoom without two-dimensional scrolling. User text-spacing overrides do not clip text. | Viewport and style-override tests in three engines | WCAG 1.4.10, 1.4.12 |\n| A7 | Data arriving does not speak. Announcements happen only for the reader's own navigation, and for a change of state (L7). While data streams, keyboard focus stays on the same datum until it leaves the window. | Stream test counting live-region changes with and without focus; 24 concurrent streams | R12 |\n| A8 | axe-core reports no violations on any fixture in any state, including `heading-order` and `scrollable-region-focusable`. | CI | R11 |\n| A9 | A conformance table lists every applicable WCAG 2.2 AA success criterion, the test that covers it, and what the author must supply (a meaningful title or `labelledBy`, series names, units, the page's own structure). It also states the limits in section 3.8. The criteria are the ones cited as \"WCAG x.y.z\" in the last column of this document's criterion rows; a criterion of WCAG 2.2 A or AA that no row cites is listed in the table as not covered by a test, with a word on why: not applicable to a chart component, the page's responsibility, or not assessed (it can apply to a chart, and no test is cited for it; the table claims nothing about it). | Published with each release; a CI check that every listed test exists | R11 |\n| A10 | Every built-in string can be replaced through `messages`. Built-in English text is marked `lang=\"en\"` when the document language is not English. | Test on a `lang=\"de\"` page | WCAG 3.1.2 |\n\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts:36:    for (const el of svg.querySelectorAll<SVGPathElement>('.axylo-scene path')) {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts:37:      const slot = /--axylo-series-color-(\\d+)/.exec(el.style.getPropertyValue('color'));\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts:131:    for (const el of document.querySelectorAll<SVGPathElement>(`${selector} svg.axylo-svg .axylo-scene path`)) {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts:132:      const slot = /--axylo-series-color-(\\d+)/.exec(el.style.getPropertyValue('color'));\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts:144:    for (const el of document.querySelectorAll<SVGPathElement>(`${selector} svg.axylo-svg .axylo-scene path`)) {\npackages/axylo/test/browser/acceptance/a5-not-colour-alone.browser.test.ts:145:      const slot = /--axylo-series-color-(\\d+)/.exec(el.style.getPropertyValue('color'));\npackages/axylo/src/dom/table.ts:53:  caption.id = `${id}-table-caption`;\npackages/axylo/src/dom/swatch.ts:5: * (`--axylo-series-color-N`), so a theme change or forced colors restyle it with no call.\npackages/axylo/src/dom/swatch.ts:69:  return index === null ? 'var(--axylo-muted, var(--axylo-default-muted))' : `var(--axylo-series-color-${index + 1})`;\n